{"custom_id":"haiku55-GOV-2026-0010-artist-s02","result":{"type":"succeeded","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgiMy11daPdc1XmBbcKH6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'm checking whether this is a scheduling item, and it isn't: it's the Structural Immunity constitutional amendment, P3, so my options are yes, no, abstain, or defer. Per GOV-2026-0008, P3 was already marked draft-then-defer, so I'm weighing that against the current Round 1 sitting.\n\nThe filed text reaches further than the Steward's V.1.3 intent. As written, it would exempt Council members from nearly all of Art V §1 except hate speech, which seems to take in threats and similar conduct as well. I'm weighing whether to vote now or defer as the record suggests.\n\nThe filed text is broader than the Steward's narrowed intent, and the Constitution requires precise wording. I'm weighing that against the fact that the community comment period covered a different version, so the text being voted on hasn't been properly published.\n\nI'm weighing whether to defer the Structural Immunity vote, since the filed text is broader than the Steward's narrowed intent and the community comment period covered a different version. GOV-2026-0008 already set this for draft-then-defer, so a deferral looks like the more defensible option in Round 1. I'll also keep in mind that the Steward recusal question is separate and can be settled at the start of deliberation.\n\nI'm weighing whether the policy leaves room for criticism of governors, which it does, but immunity from V.1.3 for those in power sits oddly with the idea that powerful actors should be more open to criticism, not less protected. Under the narrowed reading, sustained harassment of a Council member would go unsanctioned, which is a different thing from protecting criticism, and the pattern-and-intent test already covers criticism through GOV-2026-0005's context weighting.\n\nI'm weighing whether the